  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Cool weather will linger into the weekend. Near \nto slightly above normal temperatures return next week.\n\nGOES-E visible imagery and 25/17z surface analysis showed the \ndeepening Nor'easter well to the northeast of Cape Hatteras with\n1026 hPa high pressure centered over Lake Superior extending \ninto the Southeast U.S. and Deep South. Direct influences from \nthe departing Nor'easter will gradually wane this afternoon with\ngusty north/northwest winds quickly diminishing as sunset \napproaches. Tonight looks to favor a solid radiational night. \nThe boundary layer should decouple fairly quickly this evening \nand with fairly low dewpoints in place, temperatures should \nsteadily fall under clear skies. Typical for radiational nights,\nthe 25/13z NBM looks to exhibit a warm bias through daybreak\nSunday which is best highlighted by the large interquartile \nrange (as much as 4-7 degrees) at various observation sites \nacross the Lowcountry and Southeast Georgia. The coldest of the \nguidance was favored for lows tonight as a result, ranging from\nthe upper 40s/lower 50s inland to the lower 60s at the beaches,\nwith further local adjustments made to account for typical and \nwell know mesoscale influences (i.e. Charleston Harbor, Broad \nRiver, Santee-Cooper lakes, Francis Marion National Forest, \netc).\n\nTemperatures will begin to moderate Saturday as ridging builds\naloft and surface high pressure maintains its influence on the\nregion. Highs look to warm into the upper 70s/lower 80s and are\nfairly consistent with full-sun 850-1000 hPa thickness schemes.\nSaturday night into Sunday morning will likely be another cool \nnight with strong radiational cooling likely coming into play \nagain within a decoupled boundary layer. The lower side of the \nguidance envelop was favored with lows ranging from the lower \n50s well inland to the lower-mid 60s at the beaches.\n\nDry conditions look to persist into much of next week with \nbroad high pressure prevailing. Temperatures will continue to\nmoderate with near to slightly above average conditions\nexpected. The next chances for rainfall should hold off until\nThursday into Friday of next week as net moisture values begin\nto build back into the area along the backside of subtropical \nhigh pressure centered well offshore over the Atlantic.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The coastal flood risk will linger into early\nnext week.\n\nAs a nor'easter churns offshore of the Mid-Atlantic coastline, \nbreezy north-northeasterly flow will continue across the region.\nThe combination of breezy winds with the full moon today will \nresult in minor (possibly moderate) coastal flooding with each \nhigh tide cycle through the weekend (esp. for the Charleston \nHarbor).\n\nCharleston Harbor: Coastal flooding concerns will continue \ntomorrow (9/26) with the potential to reach moderate flood stage\nfor Charleston and Colleton County with the morning high tide \n(~8AM). Thereafter, it's likely to see minor coastal flooding \nconditions through early next week for the Charleston Harbor.\n\nFort Pulaski: Total water forecasts indicate tomorrow morning's\nand Sunday morning's high tide could peak around 9.5 ft MLLW, \nminor coastal flooding.",
